#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:1
msgid "The formats repository"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:3
msgid ""
"A formats repository is an ordinary directory, tracked in its own version"
" control, holding the recipes for a set of report layouts. The engine "
"reads it; it is not part of the engine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:6
msgid ""
"That separation is the project's main structural bet. Support for a new "
"report needs no release of the engine, touches nothing anyone else "
"maintains, and can be published by whoever cares about those reports. "
"`analysis_finance_reports_formats` — *Italy, five top groups* — is one "
"such repository, and there is nothing privileged about it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:11
msgid "Starting a repository"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:18
msgid "The layout of a repository"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:52
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Everything marked *generated* is rewritten between two markers — by "
"`freeports-validate report` for the grants and `freeports-dev ci-report` "
"for the figures — and what is between them is replaced whole; see {ref"
"}`what-init-format-repo-writes`. Text of your own outside the markers is "
"kept."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:56
msgid "Working on it: `make`, and asking about it: `freeports-dev`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:58
msgid ""
"The repository is worked on with `make` and interrogated with `freeports-"
"dev`, and the two are the same vocabulary rather than two ways of doing "
"the same thing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:69
msgid ""
"`make` acts on *this* repository: it writes the measurements into "
"`reports/`, refreshes the committed reports, and rewrites "
"`package.yaml`'s fingerprint when it is entitled to. `freeports-dev` and "
"`freeports-validate` act on *any* repository — `--repo ~/their-formats` —"
" and write only where you point them. That is why the same figure has two"
" commands: one to keep yours current, one to ask about somebody else's."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:75
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"{doc}`../devops/the-gate` says which targets may refuse a commit, and on "
"which branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:77
msgid "The `metadata/` tables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:78
msgid ""
"`metadata/formats.csv` lists the formats by their **components**, not by "
"their names:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:86
msgid ""
"The name every other file refers to — `AMUNDI-EN24` — is **synthesised** "
"as `Name-Locale<YY>[@Country][.Version]` and written nowhere. There is "
"exactly one place a format name comes from, so a name and its components "
"cannot disagree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:90
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"`metadata/url_mapping.csv` maps URL prefixes to format names, and is what"
" lets a document downloaded from a known address be read without "
"`--format`. When several prefixes match, the **longest** wins; between "
"equal prefixes, the row with the more specific name; after that, the row "
"that comes first. A row may give a partial name "
"({doc}`../../reference/formats/names`) — `MEDIOLANUM-IT` for an address "
"that serves every Italian Mediolanum report — and every row is checked "
"against `formats.csv` when the repository is loaded, so a name that "
"matches no format is an error at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:97
msgid ""
"`metadata/document_classify.yaml` recognises a document's format from the"
" text of its pages, for documents no URL prefix accounts for. `init-"
"format-repo` creates it with no rules; a file that is absent or empty "
"classifies nothing declaratively. When text rules are not enough, the "
"author adds `metadata/document_classify.py` — or a "
"`metadata/document_classify/` package — and nothing creates it for them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:103
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"How to write the two is {doc}`../../reference/formats/document-classify-"
"yaml` and {doc}`../../reference/formats/document-classify-python`; how "
"the names they produce are built, and which format to try when neither "
"recognises a document, is {doc}`../../reference/formats/names`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:107
msgid "Orchestration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:109
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Three tables decide what runs and in what order; "
"{doc}`../../reference/design/schedule` explains why they have this shape."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:112
msgid ""
"**`algorithms_schedule.csv`** — per format, the page classes in "
"processing order, split into steps. Classes accumulate into the current "
"step until a row raises `Filter next iteration`, which closes it and "
"opens the next. That flag is how you say \"the results so far are the "
"filter for what comes next\":"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:124
msgid ""
"Here `investments` runs first and alone; the two remaining classes run in"
" a second step and can filter on what the first found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:127
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"**`mapping.csv`** — which pipelines handle which page class, keyed by "
"`{format}({pipeline})`. Left unspecified, a page class is handled by the "
"pipeline of the same name. Several rows may point at one class, and one "
"pipeline may serve several classes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:131
msgid ""
"**`pageclassify_overwrite.csv`** — the formats that replace the standard "
"page classification with their own pipelines. Empty in the common case."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:134
msgid "Tests, and what they are for"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:136
msgid ""
"Each format under `tests/formats/<FORMAT>/` has the report, per-page "
"fixtures, and the expected output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:139
msgid ""
"The per-page fixtures — `<page>-pdf_blks.json`, `<page>-txt_blks.json`, "
"`<page>-results.json`, plus a `filter_data.json` per page class — pin one"
" page at a time and are generated by `freeports-dev make-tests`. They are"
" what tells you which segment broke."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:143
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"`out/` is different in kind. It is the **expected output of the whole "
"document**, and it is the repository's specification: if a run diverges "
"from it, the engine is what changed, not the expectation. Regenerating "
"one of those files is a deliberate act with a reason attached, never a "
"way to make a red test go green. That is also what makes the grants in "
"{doc}`../../overview/trust` meaningful — a grant is a claim about the "
"content of exactly these files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:149
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Run them with `freeports-dev test`, optionally narrowed with `--format`. "
"{doc}`dev-loop` is the workflow, {doc}`../../reference/cli/index` the "
"option-by-option reference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:152
msgid "Versioning and identity"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:154
msgid "`package.yaml` carries the repository's own identity:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/guides/formats/repository.md:164
msgid ""
"`validation_sha256` ties the repository to the validation document that "
"vouches for its contents, so that a consumer can tell not only *what* "
"they have but *what was claimed about it*."
msgstr ""

